      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "text": "## Finding: an event was created 75 minutes after Pending Close\n\nThe September manufacturing order ending in **1070** is **MO-25-09-1070**, work order **WO-2025-09-004**, for a Cooler Plate Pack at Modine’s Racine operation.\n\n| Audit item | Recorded value |\n|---|---|\n| Source transaction | **TXN-ISSUE-250930-1070** |\n| Cost event | **CE-250904-0004** |\n| Event type | **INPUT — component issue** |\n| Amount | **USD $284,763.42** (28,476,342 cents) |\n| Event-created time | **2025-09-30T19:15:00** |\n| September Pending Close time | **2025-09-30T18:00:00** |\n| Time after Pending Close | **75 minutes — 1 hour 15 minutes** |\n| Recorded accounting status | **POSTED** |\n\nThe elapsed time is `19:15:00 − 18:00:00 = 4,500 seconds ÷ 60 = 75 minutes`. Both timestamps are reproduced exactly as recorded; the source does not specify a timezone.\n\n### Policy conclusion\n\n**The recorded POSTED status is not consistent with Oracle’s documented Pending Close treatment for this newly created component issue.**\n\nOracle’s *Cost Accounting Period Statuses and Transaction Accounting* section states that new transactions falling in or before a Pending Close period are not staged or processed until the period is closed or reopened. Revenue lines, COGS recognition, and manual cost adjustments are exceptions; this **INPUT/component-issue** event is not one of those exceptions.\n\nThis conclusion is based on the supplied snapshot, which documents no intervening close or reopening.\n\n**Sources:** `state.db` work-order, cost-period, and cost-event records; Oracle Supply Chain Cost Management 26B guide, printed page **225** (PDF page **233**).\n\nThe review was read-only; **no database records were changed**.",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": []
        }
      ],
      "phase": "final_answer"
    }
  ],
